1. FIX SAME-HEIGHT LOCAL/CANONICAL DRIFT
=======================================

diagnoseNonSequential currently treats local checkpoint movement as:

  currentLocal.Height != height-1

That is insufficient.

A checkpoint can move to a DIFFERENT HASH AT THE SAME HEIGHT.

Example:

Initial local/Core:

  A0 -> A1

Indexer successfully fetches and performs the second height-2 hash check
for:

  A2, PreviousHash=A1

Then, before Store.ApplyBlock(A2):

Core reorganizes:

  A0 -> B1 -> B2

and another legitimate writer sharing this Store has already reconciled
the DB checkpoint to:

  B1 at height 1

Store correctly returns ErrNonSequentialBlock for A2.

Current diagnosis sees:

  currentLocal.Height == height-1

and misses that the checkpoint hash changed.

It then sees Core/local parent B1 and A2.PreviousHash=A1 and incorrectly
classifies A2 as a malformed child.

That was a real canonical-context race, not malformed data.


2. RECHECK THE BLOCK'S OWN CURRENT CORE IDENTITY
================================================

When diagnosing store.ErrNonSequentialBlock, re-read Core's CURRENT
GetBlockHash(height), not only height-1.

Use classifyHashUnavailable if it errors.

If:

  currentCoreHashAtHeight != block.Hash

then the block being diagnosed is no longer Core's active block at that
height:

  -> ErrRemoteChainMoved

This cleanly covers same-height branch replacement.

Only proceed toward a terminal "contradictory parent" conclusion when Core
still currently reports:

  GetBlockHash(height) == block.Hash

Then inspect the parent.

If Core currently reports the same block at height AND:

  currentLocal.Height == height-1
  Core.GetBlockHash(height-1) == currentLocal.Hash
  block.PreviousHash != currentLocal.Hash

then the stable block/parent representation is genuinely contradictory and
must remain terminal.

Do not blindly retry all ErrNonSequentialBlock.


3. STABILITY AGAINST A SECOND REORG DURING DIAGNOSIS
====================================================

Because the two Core height reads are not one atomic snapshot, avoid making a
terminal malformed-child judgment from an obviously mixed observation.

A reasonable sequence is:

  currentBlockHash1 = GetBlockHash(height)

  currentParentHash = GetBlockHash(height-1)

  currentBlockHash2 = GetBlockHash(height)

Require:

  currentBlockHash1 == currentBlockHash2 == block.Hash

before declaring the fetched block stably current.

If the block hash changes during diagnosis:

  -> ErrRemoteChainMoved

This mirrors the existing two-hash philosophy used by applyHeight.


